.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_SORT 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_qsort ,
.Nm xft_cmp_u64
.Nd in-place quicksort
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sort.h
.Ft void
.Fn xft_qsort "t_u8 *arr" "t_qsort_ctx *c" "t_size l" "t_size h"
.Ft int
.Fn xft_cmp_u64 "t_cany a" "t_cany b"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In sort.h
provides an in-place quicksort that operates on untyped bytes and takes the
element size and the comparator from a caller-supplied context.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct s_qsort_ctx {
	t_u8	*buf;		/* scratch buffer for swaps */
	t_size	size;		/* element size in bytes */
	int	(*cmp)(t_cany, t_cany);
} t_qsort_ctx;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa buf
must hold at least
.Fa size
bytes.
It is used during each swap and must not overlap the array being sorted.
.Fa cmp
returns a negative value if its first argument should be ordered before
its second, zero if they are equal, and a positive value otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn xft_qsort
sorts the
.Fa h
\-
.Fa l
elements starting at
.Fa arr
+
.Fa l
\(mu
.Fa size .
The half-open interval is the same convention as C's
.Vt qsort :
.Fa l
is the index of the first element to be sorted and
.Fa h
is one past the last.
The comparison uses a Lomuto-style partition around the element at
.Fa l ,
then recurses on both sides.
.Pp
.Fn xft_cmp_u64
is a ready-made comparator for arrays of
.Vt t_u64a .
It loads the two arguments as 64-bit words and returns
.Ql (x \*[Gt] y) - (x \*[Lt] y) ,
so the result is
.Ql \-1 ,
.Ql 0
or
.Ql 1
and never overflows.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_qsort
returns nothing.
.Fn xft_cmp_u64
returns the sign of the comparison.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Sort an array of
.Vt t_u64a
in place.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

void	sort_u64s(t_u64a *data, t_size n)
{
	t_u64a		scratch;
	t_qsort_ctx	c;

	c = (t_qsort_ctx){
		.buf = (t_u8 *)&scratch,
		.size = sizeof(t_u64a),
		.cmp = xft_cmp_u64,
	};
	xft_qsort((t_u8 *)data, &c, 0, n);
}
.Ed
.Pp
Sort an array of structures by one field.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct s_point {
	t_i32a	x;
	t_i32a	y;
} t_point;

static int	cmp_x(t_cany a, t_cany b)
{
	const t_point	*pa = a;
	const t_point	*pb = b;

	return ((pa->x > pb->x) - (pa->x < pb->x));
}

void	sort_points(t_point *pts, t_size n)
{
	t_point		scratch;
	t_qsort_ctx	c;

	c = (t_qsort_ctx){
		.buf = (t_u8 *)&scratch,
		.size = sizeof(t_point),
		.cmp = cmp_x,
	};
	xft_qsort((t_u8 *)pts, &c, 0, n);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_mem 3 ,
.Xr xft_vec 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
The sort is not stable.
Elements that compare equal may be reordered.
.Pp
.Fa buf
must hold exactly one element, not one element per recursion level; the
recursive calls share the caller's buffer.
.Pp
The interval is checked with
.Ql l + 1 >= h ,
so calling
.Fn xft_qsort
with
.Fa h
less than or equal to
.Fa l
+ 1 returns immediately.
Passing
.Fa h
equal to
.Fa l
is accepted and does nothing.
.Pp
.Fn xft_qsort
recurses on both partitions rather than iterating on one side, so the
recursion depth is linear in the number of elements for already-sorted
input.
On a large array that is already ordered this can exhaust the stack.
.Pp
.Fa cmp
must be a total order.
An inconsistent comparator gives undefined behaviour, as with C's
.Fn qsort .
